## Root cause of the verification failure

`npm run test` failed at **Jest startup** (before any test ran) with `Cannot find package 'lodash-es'`. The stack trace showed the real culprit: `next/jest` loads the bundled `next.config.js`, which (via `redirectsService` → `services/contentstack.ts`) now pulls in `@contentstack/live-preview-utils`. Node 22's require-ESM path resolves that package's **modern ESM build**, which imports several bare packages (`lodash-es`, `deepsignal`, `@preact/signals`, `dompurify`, `goober`, `get-xpath`, `mustache`, `@contentstack/advanced-post-message`, `@preact/signals-core`) that were never installed into `node_modules` because `npm install` is blocked in this environment by a private-registry 401 for `@metrolinx/cx-shared`.

## What I changed this turn

- **Installed the missing transitive dependencies** of `@contentstack/live-preview-utils` into `node_modules` by fetching exact tarballs from the public npm registry (bypassing the broken full `npm install`). Added a nested `preact@10.28.2` under `live-preview-utils/node_modules` to satisfy the `@preact/signals` peer requirement without touching the app's top-level `preact@10.7.2`.
- **`package.json`**: declared `lodash-es@4.18.1` (matching the project's existing `lodash: 4.18.1` override pin).
- **`package-lock.json`**: regenerated via `npm install --package-lock-only` (which succeeds) so the lockfile is complete and consistent for CI's `npm ci` — it resolves top-level `preact@10.29.8`, which satisfies all peers.

No application source files were changed this turn — the story implementation from the previous attempt was already correct.

